// WHAT A SPEC SECTION DEMANDS THAT COSTS MONEY — and nothing else.
//
// A GC's invitation arrives with a spec book. For a framing/drywall/plaster/
// EIFS/ceilings/fireproofing sub the money is in two or three sections of
// Division 09, and each one is thirty-odd pages of requirements written by an
// architect. The estimator's fear is specific: a requirement that costs money
// and was not in the number. Level 5 finish where Level 4 was bid. A UL
// assembly that changes the stud gauge. A field mock-up nobody priced. "No
// substitutions" on a product that costs twice the catalogue's.
//
// Until now nothing in this app had ever read a specification. An audit of the
// whole estimating workflow on 2026-10-02 confirmed it against source: no
// specification model, no reader, no `AI_FEATURES` member. The only prior hits
// anywhere were `BidAddendumReferenceKind.SPEC_SECTION` — a LABEL on an
// addendum item — and a free-text `specSection` on RFI and submittal forms.
//
// ── NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS AN ASSERTION ABOUT THE BID ──
//
// `bid-addenda.prisma`'s rule, copied rather than re-argued, because the same
// review already paid for it. No reading here writes `BidRequirement`, and that
// one deserves naming because it is the obvious thing to build: a finding like
// "submit a fire-test report for the head-of-wall assembly" is exactly the
// attestable shape `BidRequirement.OTHER` exists for.
//
// It must not, for two reasons the schema next door states itself.
// `lib/bid-responsiveness.ts` READS `BidRequirement` to decide whether a bid is
// reported non-responsive — so a model writing one would be writing a field
// another module reads, which is precisely what the addendum reader is built
// not to do. And `required Boolean @default(true)` means a model-invented row
// would default to "this bid is non-responsive without it": the destructive
// direction that killed the first addendum design, where accepting `false`
// silently removed a live warning.
//
// If a finding should ever become a requirement, it is a person pressing "add
// this as a requirement", never an accepted proposal.
//
// ── AND IT DOES NOT SAY WHETHER THE BID CARRIES THE COST ──
//
// The model is handed the section and nothing else — not the estimate, not the
// takeoff, not the catalogue. Whether a requirement is already priced is a
// judgement about numbers it has never seen, which is the same objection
// `ask/commands/estimating.ts` made when it refused `saveBidAddendum` to the
// assistant. So there is no `carried` field in this file, deliberately, and a
// reader looking for one should find this paragraph instead.

/// One section of one bid's spec book.
///
/// HANGS OFF THE INVITATION, NOT A JOB, for `pursuits.prisma`'s reason: most
/// invitations are declined or lost and never become a job, so a `jobId` here
/// would be null on nearly every row. It also keeps both models out of the
/// cleanup scripts entirely — `takeoff.prisma`'s rule, since everything here
/// CASCADEs from `BidInvitation` and nothing carries a `jobId` for
/// `clean-test-jobs.mjs` to count.
///
/// THE COST OF THAT CHOICE, STATED: a spec section is genuinely both a bid
/// document and a construction document — it arrives with the ITB and it
/// governs submittals after award. This models the bid half only. The post-award
/// use reaches it through `BidInvitation.wonJobId`, which is person-set and
/// nullable, and a job-scoped spec surface would be a different feature rather
/// than a wider version of this one.
model BidSpecSection {
  id              String        @id @default(cuid())
  bidInvitationId String
  bidInvitation   BidInvitation @relation(fields: [bidInvitationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The section number AS PRINTED — "09 21 16", "Section 09 22 16". Free text
  /// and never validated against MasterFormat, which is a decision this repo has
  /// now made five times: "a product that refuses a code for not being in a
  /// standard list is a product nobody can enter their own budget into."
  sectionNumber String
  /// What the section is called on its own first page. Null when nobody typed it.
  title         String?

  /// The PDF, and the name it was uploaded under. Both nullable: logging that
  /// section 09 21 16 is in this bid's book is useful before anybody has the
  /// file, and the `DrawingRevision` pair is the existing shape for this.
  fileUrl  String?
  fileName String?

  /// THE IN-FLIGHT CLAIM, and it is NOT the double-read window the addendum
  /// reader uses.
  ///
  /// That reader guards a double-click with `createdAt >= now - 30s` over its
  /// own readings, and `FEATURE-AUDIT.md` carries #563 open against exactly
  /// that: "the save looks like it failed and a retry duplicates it". The race
  /// is plain once named — the first read's row is not committed when the second
  /// read's check runs, so the window sees nothing and both calls bill.
  ///
  /// So this is a CLAIM instead, which is the pattern `PlanIngestTask` already
  /// proves: `updateMany` on `readInFlightAt: null` is atomic in Postgres, two
  /// racing reads produce one winner and one zero-count update, and `count === 0`
  /// IS the refusal with no window between checking and claiming.
  ///
  /// WITH AN EXPIRY, because CLAUDE.md names the alternative: "a claim column
  /// with no expiry converts a resumable job into a permanently stuck one, which
  /// is worse than not being resumable at all". A read that dies mid-call leaves
  /// this set; `SPEC_READ_LEASE_MS` is how long before another read may take it.
  readInFlightAt DateTime?

  notes String?

  readings  BidSpecReading[]
  createdAt DateTime         @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime         @updatedAt

  @@index([bidInvitationId, sectionNumber])
}

/// One read run over one section — immutable, append-only.
model BidSpecReading {
  id               String         @id @default(cuid())
  bidSpecSectionId String
  bidSpecSection   BidSpecSection @relation(fields: [bidSpecSectionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// DENORMALISED, and the query is named rather than implied: "what did reading
  /// the specs on this bid cost" and "which sections have been read" are both
  /// per INVITATION, and without this column they join through every section.
  ///
  /// THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `companyId`, the same call `BidAddendumReading`
  /// makes: it would be a third copy of one id — invitation, section, reading —
  /// and `union-tenancy-audit.mjs` exists because of that exact shape, where "a
  /// write path that sets one and forgets the other creates a row filed under
  /// company A hanging off company B's local, silently, with every constraint
  /// satisfied." Tenancy comes through the join.
  bidInvitationId String
  bidInvitation   BidInvitation @relation(fields: [bidInvitationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// WHAT THE MODEL RETURNED, exactly as it returned it.
  ///
  /// JSON RATHER THAN A CHILD TABLE, for `BidAddendumReading`'s reason: a table
  /// earns its keep when something queries or mutates one row of it, and nothing
  /// will. No screen filters findings and no action updates one — there are
  /// deliberately no per-finding decisions in this version, see below. JSON is
  /// also immutable by construction, which a column-per-field row is not.
  ///
  /// Each element: `ordinal`, `kind` (a `BidSpecFindingKind` value), `label`,
  /// `requirement` (what the section demands, in its own words), `whyItCosts`
  /// (one line an estimator can act on), `confidence`, `sourcePageLabel`, and
  /// `quote` — the sentence it was read from. The quote and the page label are
  /// what make `intake.prisma`'s standard reachable: "a reason nobody can check
  /// is a reason nobody can overrule."
  findings Json

  /// Why the reader read it the way it did, checkable against the document.
  readingReason String

  /// Pages charged against `AskAllowancePeriod.specPagesUsed`. Stored because
  /// the screen says what a re-read will cost before somebody presses it.
  pagesCharged Int

  model         String
  promptVersion String

  startedByUserId String?
  startedBy       User?   @relation(fields: [startedByUserId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  @@index([bidSpecSectionId, createdAt])
  @@index([bidInvitationId])
}

/// How findings are GROUPED on screen, and nothing more.
///
/// Diego's eight plus `GENERAL`, chosen against the six trades this product is
/// for. `GENERAL` is the deliberate escape and the reason this enum can never
/// refuse a finding — `BidAddendumReferenceKind`'s stated rule: "this enum only
/// decides how findings are grouped and can never refuse one." A section that
/// demands something none of the eight covers still produces a finding.
///
/// THERE IS NO `SUBMITTAL` MEMBER, and that is a scope decision rather than an
/// omission: Diego chose cost drivers over submittals, because what a section
/// obliges you to SUBMIT is paperwork the won job needs, while what it obliges
/// you to BUILD is money this bid either carries or does not. A submittal that
/// costs real money to prepare arrives under `TESTING` or `MOCK_UP`.
enum BidSpecFindingKind {
  /// Level 4 against Level 5, skim coat, veneer plaster.
  FINISH_LEVEL
  /// A UL assembly, a 1- or 2-hour rating, head-of-wall, firestopping.
  FIRE_RATING
  /// STC or NIC ratings, acoustic insulation, sealant at the deck.
  ACOUSTIC
  /// A sample panel or a field mock-up somebody has to build and get approved.
  MOCK_UP
  /// Third-party inspection, field quality control, testing agencies.
  TESTING
  /// "No substitutions", a sole-sourced product, a named manufacturer.
  NAMED_PRODUCT
  /// Extra material left on site at closeout.
  ATTIC_STOCK
  /// Deflection criteria (L/240, L/360), height limits, wind load.
  PERFORMANCE
  /// Anything the eight above do not cover.
  GENERAL
}
